---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'How accurate is this PTCL speed test?',
    a: 'The test measures your real connection to the nearest Cloudflare edge server. For the most accurate result, connect your computer directly to the router with an Ethernet cable, close other apps and tabs, and run the test two or three times.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is a good internet speed for PTCL broadband?',
    a: 'For one or two people browsing and streaming in HD, 10–15 Mbps is enough. For 4K streaming, online classes and video calls at the same time, aim for 25 Mbps or more. Online gaming cares more about low ping (under 50 ms) than raw speed.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why is my PTCL speed slower than my package?',
    a: 'Advertised speeds are maximums. Your real speed depends on your distance from the telephone exchange, the quality of your copper line or fibre, Wi-Fi signal strength, how many devices are connected, and peak-hour congestion.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is this speed test free?',
    a: 'Yes. The test is completely free, needs no account and no sign-up, and you can run it as many times as you like.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I test my mobile data speed here too?',
    a: 'Yes, the test measures whatever connection your device is using, including Jazz, Zong, Telenor and Ufone mobile data, and other ISPs like Nayatel and StormFiber. The advice on this site is written with PTCL in mind, but the test itself works for any connection.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How much data does a speed test use?',
    a: 'Each full test downloads and uploads roughly 100–200 MB of test data. Avoid running it repeatedly on a limited mobile data bundle, on home broadband it does not count against any cap.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the difference between Mbps and MBps?',
    a: 'Mbps means megabits per second; MBps means megabytes per second. There are 8 bits in a byte, so a 40 Mbps connection downloads at about 5 MB per second. ISPs advertise in Mbps, while browsers usually show downloads in MBps.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why is my upload speed so much slower than my download speed?',
    a: 'Most home connections are asymmetric by design, PTCL DSL packages typically offer far less upload than download, because most people download much more than they upload. Fibre (Flash Fiber) packages have much faster uploads.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What ping is good for gaming in Pakistan?',
    a: 'Under 50 ms to the game server is good; under 30 ms is great. Your ping to a nearby test server will be lower than your ping to a game server in Europe or the US, which is decided by distance. Choose Asian servers when a game offers them.',
  },
  {
    q: 'My speed is slow. Should I upgrade my package?',
    a: 'Test over an Ethernet cable first. If the wired result is close to your package speed and you still struggle, an upgrade may help. If the wired result is far below your package, fix the connection first, an upgrade on a bad line just costs more for the same speed.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does the test store my results?',
    a: 'Your test history is saved only in your own browser (localStorage), so you can compare tests over time. We never see it, and you can clear it any time from the test page. See the privacy policy for details.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do I log in to my PTCL router?',
    a: 'Connect to your PTCL WiFi, then type 192.168.10.1 into your browser address bar (older modems use 192.168.1.1). The default login is usually admin / admin, or the password printed on the router sticker. Full steps are in our PTCL router login guide.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why is the internet slow in Pakistan today?',
    a: 'As of November 2026, a fault in the SMW5 submarine cable (confirmed by the PTA on 18 November) is slowing connections nationwide, especially at peak hours, with repairs expected by 6 December 2026. Test over a cable at a quiet time to check whether it is your line or the network.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do I test my internet speed using CMD?',
    a: 'Open Command Prompt and run "ping google.com -t" for continuous ping and packet loss, or install the Ookla CLI with "winget install Ookla.Speedtest" and run "speedtest" for a full download/upload test. See our CMD speed test guide for the full steps.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the difference between PTCL DSL and Flash Fiber?',
    a: 'DSL runs over copper telephone lines (up to about 20 Mbps, slow uploads, speed depends on distance from the exchange). Flash Fiber runs over fibre to your home (30–250 Mbps, near-symmetrical uploads, very low ping). If fibre is available on your street, it is almost always the better connection.',
  },
  {
    q: 'My wired speed is below 80% of my package. What should I do?',
    a: 'First confirm it: test over an Ethernet cable at a quiet time (early morning), two or three times. If it stays below 80%, register a complaint with your provider and quote the dated results, for PTCL, call 1218 and note the complaint number.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Which is faster, PTCL or Nayatel?',
    a: 'On published averages, Nayatel is faster overall (28.3 Mbps average download vs 22.4 Mbps for PTCL\'s fixed network, nPerf 2025). But PTCL Flash Fiber on its own averages 31.7 Mbps (SpeedGeo), the fair comparison is fibre versus fibre, and availability varies street by street. See our PTCL vs Nayatel comparison for the full data.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do I fix slow Jazz internet?',
    a: 'Check your bundle first (dial *114#, a finished bundle drops your speed sharply), toggle airplane mode for 30 seconds, move near a window, lock the network mode to 4G/LTE, and check your APN settings. Full steps are in our Jazz slow internet guide.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the Jazz APN setting?',
    a: 'The safest way is to get the official settings by SMS: dial *444*6*5# on prepaid or *446*4# on postpay, or send a blank SMS to 7342. Jazz sends the settings to your phone, open the message and tap Install. See our Jazz APN settings guide for the manual values and 4G SIM checks.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What does a red LOS light mean on PTCL fibre?',
    a: 'LOS means Loss of Signal, the fibre box (ONT) is receiving no light from PTCL. Reseat the green fibre connector until it clicks and power-cycle the ONT for 30 seconds. If LOS stays red, call PTCL on 1218: the fault is in the fibre cable or PTCL\'s equipment, and restarting the router won\'t fix it.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why is my PTCL DSL light blinking?',
    a: 'A blinking DSL light means the modem is trying to sync with the exchange. Wait a few minutes after a restart; if it never goes solid, check the phone cable and splitter, confirm the landline has a dial tone, and restart the modem. A DSL light that never syncs usually means a line fault, call PTCL on 1218.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do I lower my ping for PUBG on PTCL?',
    a: 'Use an Ethernet cable, choose the nearest game server (Dubai/Middle East or Singapore), close background downloads, and switch DNS to Cloudflare (1.1.1.1) or Google (8.8.8.8). On PTCL DSL, ask PTCL on 1218 to switch your line profile to FastPath instead of Interleaved.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why is PTCL slow at night?',
    a: 'Evenings (7–11 pm) are the busiest hours on every Pakistani network, so speeds commonly dip then. Test in the morning and again at 9 pm: if mornings are fast and evenings are slow, that\'s congestion, no router setting will fix it, but both results together are strong evidence for a complaint.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is this site affiliated with PTCL?',
    a: 'No. This is an independent tool built for PTCL customers. It is not affiliated with, endorsed by, or connected to Pakistan Telecommunication Company Limited (PTCL) or Etisalat.',
  },
];

const faqSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: faqs.map((f) => ({
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: f.q,
    acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a },
  })),
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Speed Test FAQ | PTCL Speed Test"
  description="Answers about PTCL internet speed: what is a good speed, why your connection is slower than your package, ping for gaming, Mbps vs MBps, and more."
  canonical="/faq"
  schema={faqSchema}
>
  <section class="page-head">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/">Home</a> / FAQ</p>
      <h1>Frequently Asked Questions</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Everything people usually ask about PTCL speed tests and broadband speeds.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="faq-list">
        {faqs.map((f) => (
          <details class="faq">
            <summary>{f.q}</summary>
            <p>{f.a}</p>
          </details>
        ))}
      </div>
      <p class="center" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
        Still stuck? <a href="/contact">Contact us</a> or
        <a href="/guides/improve-ptcl-speed"> read the speed improvement guide</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
